meta {
  name: TTS - Speed Control Example
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/v1/tts?response_mode=url
  body: json
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  response_mode: url
}

body:json {
  {
    "text": "This example demonstrates speed control with high-quality audiostretchy library for natural sounding faster speech.",
    "reference_audio_filename": "speaker_en/jamie_vc_to_david-2.wav",
    "export_formats": ["wav", "mp3"],
    "speed_factor": 1.3,
    "speed_factor_library": "audiostretchy",
    "temperature": 0.75,
    "seed": 42
  }
}

docs {
  # Text-to-Speech - Speed Control Example
  
  Demonstrates speed adjustment using high-quality audio processing libraries.
  
  ## Speed Control Parameters
  - **speed_factor**: Playback speed multiplier (0.5-2.0, default: 1.0)
    - 1.0 = normal speed
    - 1.5 = 50% faster
    - 0.8 = 20% slower
  - **speed_factor_library**: Processing library selection
    - "auto" = Smart selection (default)
    - "audiostretchy" = Best quality, TDHS algorithm (recommended)
    - "librosa" = Good baseline compatibility
    - "torchaudio" = Basic fallback (affects pitch)
  
  ## Library Comparison
  - **audiostretchy**: Superior speech quality with formant preservation
  - **librosa**: Good quality, widely compatible
  - **torchaudio**: Affects pitch, use only as fallback
  
  ## Response Mode
  - **response_mode=url**: Returns JSON with file URLs and metadata
  
  ## Example Use Cases
  - Faster audiobook narration
  - Slower speech for learning materials
  - Adjusting pacing for different audiences
  - Time-constrained content delivery
  
  ## Performance Notes
  - Speed adjustment adds ~10-20% processing time
  - audiostretchy provides best quality for speech
  - Results are preserved in generated filename
}
